---
import { REGIONS } from '../data/costs';
---

<div>
  <h2 class="text-2xl">How much region changes the number</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-navy-600">
    National figures are a starting point, not a quote. Labour rates, permitting,
    season length, and how many crews compete locally all move the final price —
    and they move it a lot.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-200">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
            Region
          </th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
            vs national
          </th>
          <th class="py-3 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
            Why
          </th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          REGIONS.map((region) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-navy-900">{region.name}</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-semibold text-blue-600">
                {region.multiplier}
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 text-sm text-navy-500">{region.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-navy-500">
    These multipliers are directional. Treat them as a sanity check on a bid
    rather than a prediction, and remember that timing matters too — booking work
    in the off-season is often the single largest discount available.
  </p>
</div>
